---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CHANNEL_LIST, UPDATE_COPY } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="How OG Signal is made: each story is researched from the original source, drafted from a fixed set of checked facts, then checked number by number before it is published."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="page-head">
        <p class="label">About</p>
        <h1>How this site is made.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          OG Signal publishes news about artificial intelligence, language models, machine learning and
          the technology underneath them. Stories go up through the day as they are ready, and each one
          links the original source it came from.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Why it exists</h2>
        <p>
          Most AI coverage is either a press release with a headline or a thread that cannot be
          checked. The useful middle is narrow: what was announced, what the numbers actually say,
          what the vendor did not disclose, and what to watch next. That is what this site is for.
        </p>

        <h2>How a story is made</h2>
        <p>
          Every story runs through four steps in this order, and a run that fails a step publishes
          nothing.
        </p>
        <dl>
          <dt>One. Research</dt>
          <dd>
            Candidates are found across the four topics, then each one is opened at its original
            source: the company's own announcement, a filing, an official research post or a pressroom
            release. A headline on its own is not enough to proceed.
          </dd>
          <dt>Two. A list of checked facts</dt>
          <dd>
            Everything allowed into the article is written down first, as a list of statements with the
            link beside each one. If a number is not on that list it cannot appear in the article.
            Arithmetic is done and recorded at this stage.
          </dd>
          <dt>Three. Writing</dt>
          <dd>
            The article is drafted by a language model from that list of facts alone. It is given no
            other context and no ability to browse, which is what keeps it inside the facts.
          </dd>
          <dt>Four. Verification</dt>
          <dd>
            Every draft is read against that list line by line, and a script checks that each number in
            the copy appears in the list, that every cited source matches, that the length and structure
            hold, and that no hype phrases are used. Anything that fails is rejected rather than
            softened.
          </dd>
        </dl>

        <h2>What that means for you</h2>
        <p>
          The prose is machine-drafted and human-checked. The facts are sourced and dated. Each story
          shows how many claims were checked and when, and a story resting on a single primary
          announcement says so on the page instead of implying wider corroboration.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a summary service. It reports what happened and credits where it came from; it does
          not copy anyone's reporting, and it does not use other people's photographs. Where a story is
          thin, the gap is described rather than filled.
        </p>

        <h2>When it updates</h2>
        <p>
          The site is checked three times a day on IST. {UPDATE_COPY} A quiet window produces nothing:
          stories are published when there is something worth publishing, not to fill a quota. Nothing
          here is grouped by the hour it was checked, because that is a publishing schedule, not
          something a reader needs.
        </p>

        <h2>How the site is organised</h2>
        <p>
          The front page opens with the newest story and a list of what has been added today, then a
          section per topic holding that topic's latest stories. The <a href="/latest">all stories</a>
          page keeps everything, newest first. A story is listed under the topic where the substance
          sits, not where the announcement landed.
        </p>

        <h2>The four topics</h2>
        <p>
          {CHANNEL_LIST.map((c) => c.label).join(', ')}.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          Errors are corrected on the page and logged in public. The standards used are written out in
          the <a href="/editorial">editorial standards</a>, and the
          {' '}<a href="/corrections">corrections log</a> is always visible.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose dl {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
  }
  .prose dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--ink-mute);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }
  .prose dd {
    margin: 6px 0 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
